some frontline healthcare workers are  still experiencing shortages of safety  gear known as ppe about a year into the  pandemic now  a non-profit group called get us ppe  says requests from health care workers  seeking help to get the equipment  rose 260 percent between november  and december one doctor is telling us  about frustrations at her former  hospital in michigan  when she tried to make sure staff were  safe consumer investigative  correspondent anna werner joins us  and a good morning to you good morning  this doctor says she tried everything  she could to make sure nurses at her  hospital were better protected  and still she says it wasn't enough  the city of alpena in northeast michigan  sits on the shores of lake huron  with a population of just under 10 000  people  and just one hospital where doctor and  victory worked  at night it was just me in the er doc  and anything that happened in the  hospital i had to take care of and i  really liked that challenge  in april her hospital's president was  asked about preparations for the coming  pandemic  and he told a local tv station we have  enough supplies to manage  an influx of patients but that's not  what dr victory says her review as part  of the hospital's medical executive  committee found  what i saw when i actually went to the  medical floors  was that we weren't using the right  precautions  the staff wasn't safe and therefore the  patients weren't safe  then in mid-november she says the crisis  she was worrying about  hit the icu was full the medical floors  were full  and the covid unit was full of covid  patients  and she says nurses were being told to  reuse critical n95  masks by the time the hospital decided  the mask was worn out  it had been mourned way more than was  recommended and it wasn't working  we were wearing the same ass over and  over and over again  and this hospital worker whose identity  is concealed because they fear getting  fired  says staff were even afraid to ask for  new masks  we weren't testing the staff and they  were coming down with kovid  and getting sick and they were scared to  go  back to take care of more covid patients  around thanksgiving dr victory became so  concerned she says  she decided to use her own money to  purchase seven  government approved respirators called  peppers one for herself  the others to give to the nurses so i  took  all of the money that i had i spent  about seven thousand dollars and bought  seven peppers and i took them into the  hospital without the documentation  and the hospital told us not to use them  she says managers then took them away  and locked them up  it was actually taking  the protective equipment away from  people who wanted it  we wanted to know why but mid-michigan  health declined our request for an  interview  instead saying in a statement that the  safety of all our front line workers  employees volunteers and patients has  been and will always remain a top  priority  and they said they've followed ppe  standards  we asked dr shikha gupta about that  situation  sadly that's not a surprising story to  me  at this point in the pandemic dr gupta  heads the group  get us ppe which helps get medical gear  for workers  her group survey of frontline workers  found that nearly  90 percent said they are repeatedly  reusing masks  recommended to be used just once like  this hospital staff member in ohio who  asked not to be identified for fear of  losing her job  the most frustrating thing to me is  knowing that my safety is being  compromised every day  it's incredibly frustrating and as for  dr victory  on december 3rd she turned in her badge  and quit i said i can't do this  this is an unsafe situation and i can't  continue to be here  i wanted the hospital to be a safe place  for patients  and i wanted the hospital to be a safe  place to work  and it didn't seem like that was  anyone's goal  well mid michigan health told us they  are in compliance with state mandates  and cdc recommendations but gettis ppe  that group says there are still  challenges in the ppe supply chain  nationwide with some items  especially masks and gloves in  especially short supply  meanwhile gail dr victory is now working  at another hospital in a different state  again working 12-hour shifts with covid  patients  but people like dr victory are the  doctors that we really need at this  particular time thank you very much anne  i'm glad that she  is working someplace you know it's a bad  sign when a hospital worker has to  distort their voice  and and disguise their face to tell the  truth two of them about this  yeah and when a doctor has to go into  their own pocket to buy equipment they  think their colleagues need seven  thousand dollars into their own pocket  it's really distressing  when hospitals which should know about  safety precautions aren't following them  somebody got some explaining to do there
 

